WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE Seungho Yeom - OCLO: Dataset-Free Compliant Humanoid Loco-Manipulation with Dynamic Online Posture.
Synced to 03_Seungho_Yeom_OCLO_CLEAN.mp4 (talk begins at 00:00:06.006 after the intro card).

1
00:00:07.626 --> 00:00:08.886
Good afternoon, everyone.

2
00:00:09.286 --> 00:00:10.946
I'm Seungho Yeom from UCSD.

3
00:00:11.266 --> 00:00:14.586
The robot behind me is doing
nine real-world tasks,

4
00:00:14.626 --> 00:00:17.466
and all it's given
is just two hand targets.

5
00:00:17.646 --> 00:00:19.706
Let's dive into how it works.

6
00:00:24.086 --> 00:00:26.546
Humanoids take commands from many sources.

7
00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:29.666
VR teleop gives three
or more track points.

8
00:00:29.986 --> 00:00:34.206
A planner, like Workhorse,
gives five body targets.

9
00:00:34.746 --> 00:00:39.146
Scripts, policies, and agentic models,
like GPT-6 Astra, give their own.

10
00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:43.286
They differ, but everyone
says where the hands go.

11
00:00:44.766 --> 00:00:46.846
Who decides the rest of the body?

12
00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:49.406
Most controllers learn
it from human motion.

13
00:00:49.446 --> 00:00:50.446
Like SONIC.

14
00:00:50.686 --> 00:00:53.106
Others, like HOMIE,
leave it to the operator.

15
00:00:53.426 --> 00:00:56.286
We wanted the body to find
it from the hands alone,

16
00:00:56.326 --> 00:00:59.966
with no motion data,
so any source could drive it.

17
00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:04.146
In each step, I ask how a person does it.

18
00:01:04.266 --> 00:01:07.606
Like HOMIE, the policy practices
every command in simulation.

19
00:01:08.206 --> 00:01:09.666
It learns to walk first.

20
00:01:10.786 --> 00:01:14.106
Then it reaches for random hand targets.

21
00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:17.366
Each hand rides a virtual
spring and gives way.

22
00:01:17.506 --> 00:01:18.506
Like your arm.

23
00:01:18.606 --> 00:01:20.306
When someone gives you a hand,
someone bumps your cup.

24
00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:24.686
We push and pull its hands,
and the legs learn to keep balance.

25
00:01:26.666 --> 00:01:29.226
But two hand targets don't fix the body.

26
00:01:29.766 --> 00:01:32.406
When it picks up,
carries, and places a box,

27
00:01:32.446 --> 00:01:35.926
the same hands and feet
still fit many postures.

28
00:01:36.546 --> 00:01:41.026
So the last stage teaches it to follow
commands for its height and torso lean.

29
00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:44.126
Then who sets the posture?

30
00:01:44.546 --> 00:01:47.646
When you pick something up,
you don't plan your hip height.

31
00:01:47.686 --> 00:01:49.646
You just bend enough to reach.

32
00:01:49.926 --> 00:01:51.926
Our analytic prior does the same.

33
00:01:52.226 --> 00:01:54.166
Lower hands, lower pelvis.

34
00:01:54.386 --> 00:01:55.886
Farther hands, more lean.

35
00:01:57.226 --> 00:01:58.906
But the prior only checks reach.

36
00:01:59.666 --> 00:02:03.166
If a pose feels shaky,
you shift until it's steady.

37
00:02:03.506 --> 00:02:04.746
CEM does that here.

38
00:02:05.266 --> 00:02:10.426
From the live state, it simulates
64 postures one second ahead

39
00:02:10.466 --> 00:02:13.766
with the policy in the loop,
as in Sumo, and keeps the best.

40
00:02:15.706 --> 00:02:17.006
Here's the whole loop.

41
00:02:17.686 --> 00:02:18.846
Hand targets go in.

42
00:02:19.006 --> 00:02:22.826
The prior and CEM,
when needed, set the posture,

43
00:02:22.866 --> 00:02:26.606
and the policy keeps the balance
while IK moves the arms.

44
00:02:27.366 --> 00:02:32.206
And the estimated hand force goes back
into the spring, so the hands give way.

45
00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:36.606
In simulation, we test three tasks.

46
00:02:36.886 --> 00:02:41.346
Tracking hand targets while walking,
pushes on the hands, and load in one hand.

47
00:02:41.586 --> 00:02:44.606
Our prior beats CEM
alone and an end-to-end

48
00:02:44.646 --> 00:02:47.986
learned posture model on all three.

49
00:02:48.306 --> 00:02:52.266
Adding CEM to the prior works
best under pushes and loads.

50
00:02:53.986 --> 00:02:59.186
On the real G1, we compare ours with
the same training without hand forces.

51
00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:03.766
Torso pushes and pulls
barely separate the two.

52
00:03:04.006 --> 00:03:05.646
However, hand pulls do.

53
00:03:05.906 --> 00:03:10.086
At 20 to 40 newtons, a policy
train without hand targets.

54
00:03:10.206 --> 00:03:12.746
Our forces held one out of ten.

55
00:03:12.946 --> 00:03:14.866
The one without forces, five.

56
00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:15.986
And ours, ten.

57
00:03:17.326 --> 00:03:20.466
With teleop, the same
controller does nine tasks.

58
00:03:20.706 --> 00:03:25.006
Crouching to the floor,
pushing and pulling while walking.

59
00:03:25.826 --> 00:03:27.846
And keeping its hand in contact.

60
00:03:29.326 --> 00:03:30.726
Teleop is just one source.

61
00:03:31.186 --> 00:03:37.026
In simulation, a zero-shot VLM and a
fine-tuned VLA also drive our controller.

62
00:03:37.366 --> 00:03:39.406
Through the same hand targets.

63
00:03:40.026 --> 00:03:42.586
Neither handles balance or posture.

64
00:03:42.866 --> 00:03:46.266
So agentic AI would only need
to say where the hands go.

65
00:03:48.326 --> 00:03:50.966
Two workshop challenges
remain open for us.

66
00:03:51.186 --> 00:03:52.986
First, choosing contact.

67
00:03:53.306 --> 00:03:56.266
Our posture sets only height and lean.

68
00:03:56.506 --> 00:03:59.606
But it does not know where
the force should be given.

69
00:04:00.646 --> 00:04:01.806
Second, the load.

70
00:04:02.026 --> 00:04:07.486
The robot can feel it, but our prior picks
one posture for a light or heavy box.

71
00:04:10.446 --> 00:04:12.906
Let me wrap up with some take-homes.

72
00:04:13.206 --> 00:04:15.586
First, from just two hand targets.

73
00:04:16.266 --> 00:04:18.226
The coupled controller
can set its own posture

74
00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:20.766
and stay compliant with no motion data.

75
00:04:22.026 --> 00:04:26.666
Second, two hand targets could
let agentic AI drive a humanoid.

76
00:04:27.806 --> 00:04:29.066
Last but not least.

77
00:04:30.946 --> 00:04:35.166
I'm also an undergrad looking
for PhD opportunities for Fall 2027.

78
00:04:35.686 --> 00:04:39.026
If you are interested in my work,
please find me at the poster session.

79
00:04:39.226 --> 00:04:40.226
Thank you so much.

80
00:04:40.206 --> 00:04:42.806
[Applause]
